  "summary": "Distinctive subterranean water structures featuring multi-level steps and architectural ornamentation.",
  "description": "Characterized by multi-tiered, stepped corridors descending to water levels with elaborate pillared pavilions and sculptural decoration. Developed to address water scarcity while serving as social and religious spaces, reflecting regional architectural traditions.",
  "culturalContext": "The stepwells (baori/vav) of western India — Rani ki Vav (Patan, 11th c., UNESCO), Chand Baori (Abhaneri), Toorji ka Jhalra (Jodhpur) — answered arid-zone water storage with inverted temples: inverted pyramids of steps descending 13 stories to groundwater, lined with shrines, pavilions and sculpture. Water itself was sacred, so stepwells doubled as ritual bathing sites and caravan coolers; women's daily water ritual made them social architecture. Colonial piped water abandoned them; heritage tourism revived them as the most photographed vernacular architecture in India.",
